<http://dbpedia.org/resource/106_Aquarii> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**106 Aquarii**, abbreviated **106 Aqr**, is a single [star](:Star) in the [equatorial](:Celestial_equator) [constellation](:Constellation) of [Aquarius](:Aquarius_%28constellation%29). *106 Aquarii* is the [Flamsteed designation](:Flamsteed_designation), and it also bears the [Bayer designation](:Bayer_designation) **i1 Aquarii**. It has an [apparent visual magnitude](:Apparent_visual_magnitude) of +5.2, making it bright enough to be viewed from the suburbs according to the [Bortle Dark-Sky Scale](:Bortle_Dark-Sky_Scale). An annual [parallax](:Parallax) shift of 8.61 [milliarcseconds](:Milliarcsecond) yields an estimated distance of around 380 [light-years](:Light-year) (120 [parsecs](:Parsec%29) from Earth.The [spectrum](:Stellar_spectrum) of this star fits a [stellar classification](:Stellar_classification) of B9 V, indicating this is a [B-type main sequence star](:B-type_main_sequence_star). It is spinning rapidly with a [projected rotational velocity](:Projected_rotational_velocity) of 328 km/s. The star has 3 times the [mass of the Sun](:Mass_of_the_Sun) and is radiating 152 times the [Sun's luminosity](:Sun%27s_luminosity) from its [photosphere](:Photosphere) at an [effective temperature](:Effective_temperature) of 11,555 K. [X-ray emission](:X-ray_astronomy) with a luminosity of 6.0×1029 [erg](:Erg) s−1 has been detected from this star. This is unusual since a [B-type star](:B-type_star) normally does not have any significant X-ray emission. Instead, it may have an undetected lower mass companion."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://dbpedia.org/resource/R_Serpentis> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "R Serpentis"@en .
<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Mars> <http://dbpedia.org/ontology/volume> "0,15100"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#double> .
<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Scott_S._Sheppard> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Scott Sander Sheppard (born 1977) is an American astronomer and a discoverer of numerous moons, comets and minor planets in the outer Solar System. He is an astronomer in the Department of Terrestrial Magnetism at the Carnegie Institution for Science in Washington, DC. He attended Oberlin College as an undergraduate, and received his bachelor in physics with honors in 1998. Starting as a graduate student at the Institute for Astronomy at the University of Hawaii, he was credited with the discovery of many small moons of Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus, and Neptune. He has also discovered the first known trailing Neptune trojan, 2008 LC18, the first named leading Neptune trojan, 385571 Otrera, and the first high inclination Neptune trojan, 2005 TN53. These discoveries showed that the Neptune trojan"@en .
