<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Centaurus_A> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Centaurus A** (also known as **NGC 5128** or **Caldwell 77**) is a [galaxy](:Galaxy) in the [constellation](:Constellation) of [Centaurus](:Centaurus). It was discovered in 1826 by [Scottish](:Scotland) astronomer [James Dunlop](:James_Dunlop) from his home in [Parramatta](:Parramatta), in New South Wales, Australia. There is considerable debate in the literature regarding the galaxy's fundamental properties such as its [Hubble type](:Hubble_sequence) ([lenticular galaxy](:Lenticular_galaxy) or a giant [elliptical galaxy](:Elliptical_galaxy%29) and distance (10–16 million [light-years](:Light-year%29). NGC 5128 is one of the closest [radio galaxies](:Radio_galaxy) to [Earth](:Earth), so its [active galactic nucleus](:Active_galactic_nucleus) has been extensively studied by professional astronomers. The galaxy is also the [fifth-brightest](:List_of_galaxies%23Naked-eye_galaxies) in the sky, making it an ideal amateur astronomy target. It is only visible from the southern hemisphere and low northern latitudes.The center of the galaxy contains a [supermassive black hole](:Supermassive_black_hole) with a mass of 55 million [solar masses](:Solar_mass), which ejects a [relativistic jet](:Astrophysical_jet%23Relativistic_jet) that is responsible for emissions in the [X-ray](:X-ray) and [radio](:Radio_spectrum) wavelengths. By taking radio observations of the jet separated by a decade, astronomers have determined that the inner parts of the jet are moving at about half of the [speed of light](:Speed_of_light). X-rays are produced farther out as the jet collides with surrounding gases, resulting in the creation of highly energetic particles. The X-ray jets of Centaurus A are thousands of light-years long, while the radio jets are over a million light-years long.As in other [starburst galaxies](:Starburst_galaxy), a [galactic collision](:Galaxy_merger) is suspected to be responsible for an intense burst of star formation. Models have suggested that Centaurus A was a large elliptical galaxy that collided with a smaller spiral galaxy, with which it will eventually merge. For that reason, the galaxy has been of particular interest to astronomers for years. While collisions of spiral galaxies are relatively common, the effects of a collision between an elliptical and a spiral galaxy are not fully understood."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
