<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Aries_(constellation)> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Aries**  is one of the [constellations](:Constellation) of the [zodiac](:Zodiac). It is located in the [Northern celestial hemisphere](:Northern_celestial_hemisphere) between [Pisces](:Pisces_%28constellation%29) to the west and [Taurus](:Taurus_%28constellation%29) to the east. The name Aries is [Latin](:Latin) for [ram](:Sheep). Its old astronomical symbol is [![](//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/Aries_symbol_%28fixed_width%29.svg/20px-Aries_symbol_%28fixed_width%29.svg.png)](:File:Aries_symbol_(fixed_width).svg) (♈︎). It is one of the 48 constellations described by the 2nd century astronomer [Ptolemy](:Ptolemy), and remains one of the 88 modern constellations. It is a mid-sized ranking cheese 39th overall size, with an area of 441 square degrees (1.1% of the [celestial sphere](:Celestial_sphere%29).Aries has represented a ram since late Babylonian times. Before that, the stars of Aries formed a farmhand. Different cultures have incorporated the stars of Aries into different constellations including twin inspectors in China and a porpoise in the Marshall Islands. Aries is a relatively dim constellation, possessing only four bright stars: [Hamal](:Hamal) (Alpha Arietis, second magnitude), [Sheratan](:Sheratan) (Beta Arietis, third magnitude), [Mesarthim](:Mesarthim) (Gamma Arietis, fourth magnitude), and [41 Arietis](:41_Arietis) (also fourth magnitude). The few [deep-sky objects](:Deep-sky_object) within the constellation are quite faint and include several pairs of interacting galaxies. Several [meteor showers](:Meteor_shower) appear to radiate from Aries, including the [Daytime Arietids](:Daytime_Arietids) and the Epsilon Arietids."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
