<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Mars> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Mars** is the fourth [planet](:Planet) from the [Sun](:Sun) and the second-smallest planet in the [Solar System](:Solar_System), being larger than only [Mercury](:Mercury_%28planet%29). In the English language, Mars is named for the [Roman god of war](:Mars_%28mythology%29). Mars is a [terrestrial planet](:Terrestrial_planet) with a thin [atmosphere](:Atmosphere) (less than 1% that of Earth's), and has a crust primarily composed of elements similar to Earth's crust, as well as a core made of iron and nickel. Mars has surface features such as [impact craters](:Impact_crater), valleys, dunes, and [polar ice caps](:Polar_ice_caps). It has two small and irregularly shaped moons: [Phobos](:Phobos_%28moon%29) and [Deimos](:Deimos_%28moon%29).Some of the most notable surface features on Mars include [Olympus Mons](:Olympus_Mons), the largest [volcano](:Volcano) and [highest known mountain](:List_of_tallest_mountains_in_the_Solar_System) on any planet in the Solar System, and [Valles Marineris](:Valles_Marineris), one of the largest canyons in the Solar System. The [Borealis basin](:North_Polar_Basin_%28Mars%29) in the Northern Hemisphere covers approximately 40% of the planet and may be a large impact feature. [Days and seasons on Mars](:Timekeeping_on_Mars) are comparable to those of Earth, as the planets have a similar [rotation period](:Rotation_period) and tilt of the [rotational axis](:Rotation_around_a_fixed_axis) relative to the [ecliptic plane](:Ecliptic). Liquid [water on the surface of Mars](:Water_on_Mars) cannot exist due to low atmospheric pressure, which is less than 1% of the atmospheric pressure on Earth. Both of Mars's polar ice caps appear to be made largely of water. In the distant past, Mars was likely wetter, and thus possibly more suited for life. However, it is unknown whether [life has ever existed on Mars](:Life_on_Mars).[Mars has been explored](:Exploration_of_Mars) by several uncrewed spacecraft, beginning with *[Mariner 4](:Mariner_4)* in 1965. NASA's *[Viking 1](:Viking_program)* lander transmitted in 1976 the first images from the Martian surface. Two countries have successfully deployed [rovers](:Rover_%28space_exploration%29) on Mars, the United States first doing so with *[Sojourner](:Sojourner_%28rover%29)* in 1997 and China with *[Zhurong](:Zhurong_%28rover%29)* in 2021. There are also planned future missions to Mars, such as a [Mars sample-return mission](:Mars_sample-return_mission) set to happen in 2026, and the [*Rosalind Franklin* rover mission](:Rosalind_Franklin_%28rover%29), which was intended to launch in 2018 but was delayed to 2024 at the earliest, with a more likely launch date at 2028.Mars can be viewed from Earth with the naked eye, as can its [reddish coloring](:Mars_surface_color). This appearance, due to the [iron oxide](:Iron%28III)_oxide) prevalent on its surface, has led to Mars often being called the **Red Planet**. It is among the [brightest objects in Earth's sky](:List_of_brightest_natural_objects_in_the_sky), with an [apparent magnitude](:Apparent_magnitude) that reaches −2.94, comparable to that of [Jupiter](:Jupiter) and surpassed only by [Venus](:Venus), the [Moon](:Moon) and the Sun. Historically, Mars has been observed since ancient times, and over the millennia has been featured in culture and the arts in ways that have reflected humanity's growing knowledge of it."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
