<http://dbpedia.org/resource/35_Comae_Berenices> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**35 Comae Berenices** is a [multiple star system](:Multiple_star_system) in the northern [constellation](:Constellation) of [Coma Berenices](:Coma_Berenices), located about 6° from the [north galactic pole](:North_galactic_pole). It is faintly visible to the naked eye with an [apparent visual magnitude](:Apparent_visual_magnitude) of 4.93. Based upon [parallax](:Stellar_parallax) measurements, it is located around 280 [light years](:Light_year) from the Sun. The system is moving closer to the Earth with a heliocentric [radial velocity](:Radial_velocity) of −6 km/s.This was found to be a wide binary by [Struve](:Friedrich_Georg_Wilhelm_von_Struve) in 1828, but it has completed less than half an orbit since then and the orbital elements remain poorly constrained. It has an [orbital period](:Orbital_period) of 539±95 years and an [eccentricity](:Orbital_eccentricity) of 0.2±0.1. The pair have been resolved into [stellar classifications](:Stellar_classification) of an [evolved](:Stellar_evolution) [G-type](:G-type_star) [giant](:Giant_star) primary of class G5 III and an [F-type](:F-type_star) secondary, most likely [main sequence](:Main_sequence) with a class of F1 V. The primary component is a [spectroscopic binary](:Spectroscopic_binary) with a period of 7.9624 ± 0.0117 years and an eccentricity of 0.63. A fourth component, 35 Com C, is located 29[″](:Second_of_arc) from the primary and may have a physical association."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Formation_and_evolution_of_the_Solar_System> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#seeAlso> <http://dbpedia.org/resource/Giant-impact_hypothesis> .
<http://dbpedia.org/resource/1176_Lucidor> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "1176 Lucidor, provisional designation 1930 VE, is a carbonaceous background asteroid from the central region of the asteroid belt, approximately 30 kilometers in diameter. It was discovered by Eugène Delporte in 1930, who named it after a friend."@en .
