<http://dbpedia.org/resource/15_Arietis> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**15 Arietis** (abbreviated **15 Ari**) is a single [variable star](:Variable_star) in the northern [constellation](:Constellation) of [Aries](:Aries_%28constellation%29). *15 Arietis* is the [Flamsteed designation](:Flamsteed_designation); it also bears the [variable star designation](:Variable_star_designation) **AV Arietis**. It has an [apparent visual magnitude](:Apparent_visual_magnitude) of 5.74, which is just bright enough to be visible to the naked eye from [dark suburban skies](:Bortle_Dark-Sky_Scale). An annual [parallax](:Parallax) shift of 5.84 [mas](:Milliarcsecond) corresponds to a physical distance of approximately 560 [light-years](:Light-year) (170 [parsecs](:Parsec%29) from Earth. At that distance, the star's brightness is reduced by 0.33 in magnitude because of [extinction](:Extinction_%28astronomy%29) from [interstellar gas and dust](:Interstellar_medium).This is a [red giant](:Red_giant) star with a [stellar classification](:Stellar_classification) of M3 III. The measured [angular diameter](:Angular_diameter) of this star is 3.67 ± 0.11 mas. At the estimated distance of Delta Ophiuchi, this yields a physical size of about 67 times the [radius of the Sun](:Solar_radius). The radius determined from the observed brightness and colour of the star is 87 R☉.15 Arietis is a [short period semiregular variable](:Semiregular_variable) with the [designation](:Variable_star_designation) AV Arietis. The period given in the [General Catalogue of Variable Stars](:General_Catalogue_of_Variable_Stars) is 5.032 days. Longterm photometry finds that the strongest pulsation period is 18.1 days with an amplitude of 0.028 magnitudes, while a second is 21.9 days and 0.030 in magnitude."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
