<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Theta1_Orionis_A> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Theta1 Orionis A (θ1 Ori A) is a variable trinary star in the constellation Orion. Its apparent magnitude range is 6.72 to 7.65 with a period of 65.432 days. It is one of the main stars in The Trapezium in Orion, along with B, C, and D, as well as the fainter E."@en .
<http://dbpedia.org/resource/CoRoT> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q2133344> .
<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Rho_Tauri> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Rho Tauri** (ρ Tau, ρ Tauri) is a [star](:Star) in the [constellation](:Constellation) [Taurus](:Taurus_%28constellation%29) and a member of the [Hyades](:Hyades_%28star_cluster%29) [star cluster](:Open_cluster).Rho Tauri is a white [A-type](:Stellar_classification) [main sequence dwarf](:Main_sequence) with a mean [apparent magnitude](:Apparent_magnitude) of +4.66. It is approximately 158 [light years](:Light_years) from [Earth](:Earth). Classified as a [Delta Scuti type](:Delta_Scuti_variable) [variable star](:Variable_star), its brightness varies by 0.010 magnitudes over a period of 1.61 hours.It has 1.88 times the [mass of the Sun](:Mass_of_the_Sun), with a [projected rotational velocity](:Projected_rotational_velocity) of 117 km/s and an estimated rotation period of 488.5 days."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://dbpedia.org/resource/NGC_2441> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**NGC 2441** is a [barred spiral galaxy](:Barred_spiral_galaxy) located in the northern constellation of [Camelopardalis](:Camelopardalis_%28constellation%29). A [Type 1a supernova](:Type_1a_supernova), **SN1995E**, occurred in NGC 2441, and observations suggest it may display a [light echo](:Light_echo), where light from the supernova is reflected from matter along our line of sight, making it appear to \"echo\" outwards from the source. The diameter of the galaxy is about 130,000 light-years."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
