<http://dbpedia.org/resource/NGC_3632> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "NGC 3632 (also known as Caldwell 40) and NGC 3626 is an unbarred lenticular galaxy and Caldwell object in the constellation Leo. It was discovered by William Herschel, on 14 March 1784. It shines at magnitude +10.6/+10.9. Its celestial coordinates are RA 11h 20.1m , dec +18° 21′. It is located near the naked-eye-class A4 star Zosma, as well as galaxies NGC 3608, NGC 3607, , NGC 3686, , , , and . Its dimensions are 2′.7 × 1′.9. The galaxy belongs to the NGC 3607 group some 70 million light-years distant, itself one of the many Leo II groups."@en .
<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Grand_Duchy_of_Baden> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://dbpedia.org/ontology/Place> .
<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Eta_Aquarii> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Eta Aquarii**, [Latinized](:Latinisation_of_names) from η Aquarii, is the [Bayer designation](:Bayer_designation) for a [star](:Star) in the [equatorial](:Celestial_equator) [constellation](:Constellation) of [Aquarius](:Aquarius_%28constellation%29). It is visible to the naked eye with an [apparent visual magnitude](:Apparent_visual_magnitude) of 4.04. The distance to this star, as determined by [parallax](:Parallax) measurements, is about 168 [light-years](:Light-year) (52 [parsecs](:Parsec%29). It is drifting closer to the [Sun](:Sun) with a [radial velocity](:Radial_velocity) of –8 km/s. Eta Aquarii is near the radiant of a meteor shower [named after it](:Eta_Aquariids).The [stellar classification](:Stellar_classification) of Eta Aquarii is B9IV-Vn, which may indicate that it is beginning to [evolve](:Stellar_evolution) away from the [main sequence](:Main_sequence) into a [subgiant](:Subgiant_star) as the supply of hydrogen at its core becomes exhausted. It is 65 million years old and is spinning rapidly with a high [projected rotational velocity](:Projected_rotational_velocity) of 271 km/s. This rotation is causing an equatorial bulge, giving the star an oblate figure with a 24% larger radius at the equator than at the poles. The [Doppler effect](:Doppler_effect) from the rapid rotation is causing the [absorption lines](:Absorption_line) in the star's [spectrum](:Stellar_spectrum) to blur, as indicated by the 'n' suffix in the star's classification. Eta Aquarii has an estimated 2.96 times the [mass of the Sun](:Mass_of_the_Sun) and 2.8 times the [Sun's radius](:Sun%27s_radius). The star is radiating 103 times the [luminosity of the Sun](:Luminosity_of_the_Sun) from its [photosphere](:Photosphere) at an [effective temperature](:Effective_temperature) of 11,042 K."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://dbpedia.org/resource/University_of_Hertfordshire> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q3918> .
<http://dbpedia.org/resource/HD_46568> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**HD 46568** (**HR 2399**) is a solitary [star](:Star) in the southern [constellation](:Constellation) [Columba](:Columba_%28constellation%29). It is faintly visible to the [naked eye](:Naked_eye) with an [apparent magnitude](:Apparent_magnitude) of 5.25. [Parallax](:Parallax) measurements place the object at a distance of 284 [light years](:Light_year) and is currently receding with a heliocentric [radial velocity](:Radial_velocity) of 39 [km/s](:Metre_per_second).HD 46568 has a [stellar classification](:Stellar_classification) of G8 III, indicating that it is a [yellow giant](:Yellow_giant). At present it has nearly twice the [mass of the Sun](:Mass_of_the_Sun) but at an age of 1.68 [billion years](:Billion_years) it has expanded to 10.66 times the [radius of the Sun](:Radius_of_the_Sun). It has an [effective temperature](:Effective_temperature) of 4,935 [K](:Kelvin), giving it a yellow glow. However, the star's large radius yields a [luminosity](:Luminosity) 60 times that of [Sun](:Sun). HD 46568's [metallicity](:Metallicity) is 69% that of the Sun and it spins with a poorly constrained [projected rotational velocity](:Projected_rotational_velocity) of 1.2 [km/s](:Metre_per_second)."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
