<http://dbpedia.org/resource/HD_49947> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**HD 49947** (**HR 2531**) is a solitary [star](:Star) in the southern [circumpolar constellation](:Circumpolar_constellation) [Volans](:Volans). It has an apparent magnitude of 6.36, placing it near the max [naked eye](:Naked_eye) visibility. [Parallax](:Parallax) measurements place the object at a distance of 459 [light years](:Light_year) but is receding with a heliocentric radial velocity of 15.88 [km/s](:Metre_per_second)HD 49947 has a [stellar classification](:Stellar_classification) of G8 III and is a [red clump](:Red_clump) giant, meaning that it is located on the warm end of the [horizontal branch](:Horizontal_branch). At present it has double the [mass of the Sun](:Mass_of_the_Sun) and at an age of 1.27 [billion years](:Billion_years), has expanded to an enlarged [radius](:Radius) of 10.33 [solar radii](:Solar_radii). It radiates at 61 times the [luminosity of the Sun](:Luminosity_of_the_Sun) from its enlarged [photosphere](:Photosphere) at an [effective temperature](:Effective_temperature) of 4,964 [K](:Kelvin), giving a yellow hue. HD 49947 is metal deficient with an [iron](:Iron) abundance 68% that of [Sun](:Sun) and spins very slowly."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://dbpedia.org/resource/HD_37124_c> <http://dbpedia.org/ontology/discoverer> <http://dbpedia.org/resource/Geoffrey_Marcy> .
<http://dbpedia.org/resource/37519_Amphios> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**37519 Amphios**  is a [Jupiter trojan](:Jupiter_trojan) from the [Trojan camp](:Trojan_camp), approximately 33 kilometers (21 miles) in diameter. It was discovered at the [Palomar Observatory](:Palomar_Observatory) during the third [Palomar–Leiden Trojan survey](:Palomar%E2%80%93Leiden_Trojan_survey) in 1977. The dark Jovian [asteroid](:Asteroid) is a member of an [unnamed asteroid family](:Asteroid_family%23010) and has a long [rotation period](:Rotation_period) of 50.9 hours. It was named after [Amphius](:Amphius) from Greek mythology."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://dbpedia.org/resource/CoRoT> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Thing> .
<http://dbpedia.org/resource/MOA-2007-BLG-197Lb> <http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/name> "MOA-2007-BLG-197Lb"@en .
<http://dbpedia.org/resource/CoRoT> <http://dbpedia.org/ontology/operator> <http://dbpedia.org/resource/Centre_National_d'Etudes_Spatiales> .
<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Sigma_Scorpii> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Sigma Scorpii** (or **σ Scorpii**, abbreviated **Sigma Sco** or **σ Sco**), is a [multiple star system](:Multiple_star_system) in the [constellation](:Constellation) of [Scorpius](:Scorpius), located near the red supergiant [Antares](:Antares), which outshines it. This system has a combined [apparent visual magnitude](:Apparent_visual_magnitude) of +2.88, making it one of the [brighter members](:List_of_stars_in_Scorpius) of the constellation. Based upon [parallax](:Stellar_parallax) measurements made during the [Hipparcos](:Hipparcos) mission, the distance to Sigma Scorpii is roughly 696 [light-years](:Light-year) (214 [parsecs](:Parsec%29). North *et al.* (2007) computed a more accurate estimate of 568+75  −59 light years (174+23  −18 parsecs).The system consists of a [spectroscopic binary](:Spectroscopic_binary) with components designated Sigma Scorpii Aa1 (officially named **Alniyat** , the traditional name for the entire star system) and a [Beta Cephei variable](:Beta_Cephei_variable%29) and Aa2; a third component (designated Sigma Scorpii Ab) at 0.4 [arcseconds](:Arcsecond) from the spectroscopic pair, and a fourth component (Sigma Scorpii B) at about 20 arcseconds."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Leiden_Observatory> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Thing> .
