<http://dbpedia.org/resource/HD_70930> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**HD 70930** is a [binary star](:Binary_star) system in the southern [constellation](:Constellation) of [Vela](:Vela_%28constellation%29). It has the [Bayer designation](:Bayer_designation) *B Velorum*, while *HD 70930* is the star's identifier in the *[Henry Draper catalogue](:Henry_Draper_catalogue)*. With a combined [apparent visual magnitude](:Apparent_visual_magnitude) of 4.79, it is visible to the naked eye as a faint point of light. The distance to this system is approximately 1,700 [light years](:Light_year) based on [parallax](:Stellar_parallax), and it has an [absolute magnitude](:Absolute_magnitude) of −3.74. It is drifting further away from the [Sun](:Sun) with a [radial velocity](:Radial_velocity) of about +27 km/s. The system is a member of the Vel OB2 association of co-moving stars.The double nature of this system was discovered in 1896 by Scottish astronomer [Robert T. A. Innes](:Robert_T%2E_A%2E_Innes) – it is now known to be a double-lined [spectroscopic binary](:Spectroscopic_binary). The magnitude 5.14 primary component has a blue-white hue and has been assigned [stellar classifications](:Stellar_classification) of B1V and B2III, matching a [B-type main-sequence star](:B-type_main-sequence_star) or a [giant star](:Giant_star), respectively. It is a massive object – over 15 times the [mass of the Sun](:Mass_of_the_Sun) – and is around 10 million years old. The star has a high rate of spin, showing a [projected rotational velocity](:Projected_rotational_velocity) of 169 km/s. Its companion, at magnitude +6.08, is located at an [angular separation](:Angular_separation) of 0.8[″](:Second_of_arc) along a [position angle](:Position_angle) of 139°, as of 2008."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://dbpedia.org/resource/CoRoT-22b> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "CoRoT-22b"@en .
<http://dbpedia.org/resource/NGC_2441> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://dbpedia.org/ontology/Place> .
<http://dbpedia.org/resource/2014_LY21> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://dbpedia.org/ontology/CelestialBody> .
<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Zeta_Pavonis> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Zeta Pavonis, Latinized from ζ Pavonis, is an orange-hued star in the southern constellation Pavo. Its apparent magnitude is 4.01, which is bright enough to be faintly visible to the naked eye. The annual parallax shift of this star is 14.93 mas as seen from Earth, which provides a distance estimate of approximately 420 light-years (130 parsecs) away from the Sun. It is moving closer to the Sun with a radial velocity of −16.30. Based upon its motion through space, this star appears to be a member of the Hyades Supercluster."@en .
<http://dbpedia.org/resource/S_Normae> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Thing> .
<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Leo_Königsberger> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q215627> .
<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Cornelis_Johannes_van_Houten> <http://dbpedia.org/ontology/academicDiscipline> <http://dbpedia.org/resource/Astronomy> .
<http://dbpedia.org/resource/HD_11506> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**HD 11506** is a [star](:Star) in the [equatorial](:Celestial_equator) [constellation](:Constellation) of [Cetus](:Cetus). It has a yellow hue and can be viewed with a small telescope but is too faint to be visible to the naked eye, having an [apparent visual magnitude](:Apparent_visual_magnitude) of 7.51. The distance to this object is 167 [light years](:Light_year) based on [parallax](:Stellar_parallax), but it is drifting closer to the [Sun](:Sun) with a [radial velocity](:Radial_velocity) of −7.5 km/s. It has an [absolute magnitude](:Absolute_magnitude) of 3.94.This object is an ordinary [G-type main-sequence star](:G-type_main-sequence_star) with a [stellar classification](:Stellar_classification) of G0V, which indicates it is generating energy via [hydrogen fusion](:Hydrogen_fusion) at its [core](:Stellar_core). It is around 1.6 billion years old and is spinning with a [projected rotational velocity](:Projected_rotational_velocity) of 5 km/s. The star has 112% of the [mass of the Sun](:Mass_of_the_Sun) and 106% of the [Sun's radius](:Sun%27s_radius). The [spectrum](:Stellar_spectrum) shows a higher than solar abundance of elements other than hydrogen and helium – what astronomers term the [metallicity](:Metallicity). The star is radiating 117% of the [Sun's luminosity](:Sun%27s_luminosity) from its [photosphere](:Photosphere) at an [effective temperature](:Effective_temperature) of 5,833 K."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
