<http://dbpedia.org/resource/BL_Boötis> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**BL Boötis** (abbreviated to **BL Boo**) is a [pulsating star](:Pulsating_star) in the constellation [Boötes](:Bo%C3%B6tes). It is the prototype of a class of anomalous [Cepheids](:Cepheids) which is intermediate in the [H-R diagram](:H-R_diagram) between the type I [classical Cepheids](:Classical_Cepheid) and the [type II Cepheids](:Type_II_Cepheid).It varies from magnitude 14.45 to 15.10 over 0.82 days. It is located 4 arcminutes from the centre of (and assumed to be a member star of) the [globular cluster](:Globular_cluster) [NGC 5466](:NGC_5466). Its variability was first noted in 1961 by Russian astronomer Nikolaĭ Efimovich Kurochkin, who gave it the variable star designation BL Boötis. However, he thought it was an [eclipsing binary](:Eclipsing_binary). It was subsequently thought to be an [RR Lyrae variable](:RR_Lyrae_variable) by T.I. Gryzunova in 1971.Robert Zinn confirmed it was a member of the globular cluster and found it was too blue to be an [RR Lyrae variable](:RR_Lyrae_variable). He gave it the name V19 within the cluster. He calculated its [mass](:Solar_mass) to be around 1.56 times and its [luminosity](:Luminosity) to be around 278 times that of the Sun; its [absolute magnitude](:Absolute_magnitude) is -1.27. The [spectrum](:Stellar_spectrum) has been compared to an A2 or A3 [main sequence](:Main_sequence) star on the basis of its [K lines](:Calcium_K_line) and A9 or F0 on the basis of its hydrogen lines. The discrepancy is due to a strong deficiency in metals, around 100 times lower than that of the [Sun](:Sun). Despite the spectral classification, it is not thought to be a main-sequence star, at least not a normal one. It is larger and more luminous than its spectrum would indicate, comparable to a [horizontal branch](:Horizontal_branch) star but more massive than comparable stars on the horizontal branch. The pulsations are thought to be in the [first overtone](:Overtone).BL Boötis has been designated the prototype of a rare class of variable star known as an [anomalous Cepheid](:Anomalous_Cepheid) or BL Boötis variable. These stars are somewhat similar to [Cepheid variables](:Cepheid_variable), but they do not have the same relationship between their period and luminosity. Their periods are similar to the ab subtypes of RR Lyrae variables; however, they are far brighter than these stars. Anomalous Cepheids are [metal poor](:Metallicity) and have masses not much larger than the Sun's, on average, 1.5 [solar masses](:Solar_mass). The origin of these stars is uncertain, but thought to possibly be from the merger of two stars. Detailed examination of the spectrum of BL Boötis with the Keck-1 telescope at the [W. M. Keck Observatory](:W%2E_M%2E_Keck_Observatory) showed that its [effective (surface) temperature](:Effective_temperature) is around 6450 K at minimum light. It also showed that the chemical composition was consistent with ageing metal-poor (Population II) stars and hence cast doubt on the origin as a result of a stellar merger. The radial velocity is lower than would be expected if it were from a stellar merger."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
