<http://dbpedia.org/resource/NGC_442> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "NGC 442 is a spiral galaxy of type S0/a? (edge on) located in the constellation Cetus. Lewis Swift discovered it on October 21, 1886. Dreyer first described it as \"very faint, small, round, bright star to southeast.\" The star is actually located northeast of NGC 442, but, due to the way optical telescopes worked, it was not unusual for some confusion of directions to occur."@en .
<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Gamma_Hydri> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**γ Hydri**, Latinised as **Gamma Hydri**, is a solitary, red-hued [star](:Star) in the [constellation](:Constellation) [Hydrus](:Hydrus_%28constellation%29). It has an [apparent visual magnitude](:Apparent_visual_magnitude) of 3.26, making it easily visible to the naked eye at night. Based upon an annual [parallax shift](:Stellar_parallax) of 15.24 [mas](:Milliarcsecond) as measured from Earth, the system is located about 214 [light-years](:Light-year) from the [Sun](:Sun).This is an [evolved](:Stellar_evolution) [red giant](:Red_giant) star with a [stellar classification](:Stellar_classification) of M1 III, and is most likely on the [asymptotic giant branch](:Asymptotic_giant_branch) of the [HR diagram](:HR_diagram). It is a [semiregular variable](:Semiregular_variable) that pulsates between magnitudes 3.26 and 3.33, although its period is not known precisely. It has about the same [mass as the Sun](:Solar_mass), but has expanded to around 62 times the [Sun's radius](:Sun%27s_radius). The star is radiating 513 times the [Sun's luminosity](:Sun%27s_luminosity) from its enlarged [photosphere](:Photosphere) at an [effective temperature](:Effective_temperature) of 3,499 [K](:Kelvin)."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
