<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Jupiter> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Jupiter** is the fifth [planet](:Planet) from the [Sun](:Sun) and the [largest in the Solar System](:List_of_Solar_System_objects_by_size). It is a [gas giant](:Gas_giant) with a [mass](:Mass) more than two and a half times that of all the other planets in the [Solar System](:Solar_System) combined, but slightly less than one-thousandth the mass of the Sun. Jupiter is the [third brightest natural object](:List_of_brightest_natural_objects_in_the_sky) in the [Earth](:Earth)'s night sky after the [Moon](:Moon) and [Venus](:Venus), and it has been observed since [prehistoric times](:Pre-history). It was named after the [Roman god Jupiter](:Jupiter_%28mythology%29), the king of the gods.Jupiter is primarily composed of [hydrogen](:Hydrogen), but [helium](:Helium) constitutes one-quarter of its mass and one-tenth of its volume. It probably has a rocky core of heavier elements, but, like the other [giant planets](:Giant_planet) in the Solar System, it lacks a well-defined solid surface. The ongoing contraction of Jupiter's interior generates more heat than it receives from the Sun. Because of its rapid rotation, the planet's shape is an [oblate spheroid](:Oblate_spheroid): it has a slight but noticeable bulge around the equator. The outer atmosphere is divided into a series of latitudinal bands, with turbulence and storms along their interacting boundaries. A prominent result of this is the [Great Red Spot](:Great_Red_Spot), a giant storm which has been observed since at least 1831.Jupiter is surrounded by a faint [planetary ring](:Planetary_ring) system and a powerful [magnetosphere](:Magnetosphere). Jupiter's [magnetic tail](:Magnetic_tail) is nearly 800 million [km](:Kilometre) (5.3 [AU](:Astronomical_unit); 500 million [mi](:Mile%29) long, covering nearly the entire distance to [Saturn](:Saturn)'s orbit. Jupiter has [80 known moons](:Moons_of_Jupiter) and possibly many more, including the [four large moons](:Galilean_moons) discovered by [Galileo Galilei](:Galileo_Galilei) in 1610: [Io](:Io_%28moon%29), [Europa](:Europa_%28moon%29), [Ganymede](:Ganymede_%28moon%29), and [Callisto](:Callisto_%28moon%29). Io and Europa are about the size of Earth's Moon; Callisto is almost the size of the planet [Mercury](:Mercury_%28planet%29), and Ganymede is larger.*[Pioneer 10](:Pioneer_10)* was the first spacecraft to visit Jupiter, making its closest approach to the planet in December 1973. Jupiter has since been [explored](:Exploration_of_Jupiter) by multiple [robotic spacecraft](:Robotic_spacecraft), beginning with the *[Pioneer](:Pioneer_program)* and *[Voyager](:Voyager_program)* [flyby](:Planetary_flyby) missions from 1973 to 1979, and later with the [*Galileo* orbiter](:Galileo_%28spacecraft%29) in 1995. In 2007, the *[New Horizons](:New_Horizons)* visited Jupiter [using its gravity](:Gravitational_slingshot) to increase its speed, bending its trajectory en route to [Pluto](:Pluto). The latest probe to visit the planet, *[Juno](:Juno_%28spacecraft%29)*, entered orbit around Jupiter in July 2016. Future targets for exploration in the Jupiter system include the probable ice-covered liquid ocean of Europa."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
