<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Xi2_Centauri> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Xi2 Centauri**, [Latinized](:Latinisation_of_names) from ξ2 Centauri, is a [triple star](:Triple_star) system in the southern [constellation](:Constellation) of [Centaurus](:Centaurus). It is visible to the naked eye with an [apparent visual magnitude](:Apparent_visual_magnitude) of 4.30, and forms a wide [optical double](:Optical_double) with the slightly dimmer [ξ1 Centauri](:Xi1_Centauri). Based upon an annual [parallax shift](:Stellar_parallax) of 6.98 [mas](:Milliarcsecond), Xi2 Centauri lies roughly 470 [light years](:Light_year) from the [Sun](:Sun). At that distance, the visual magnitude is diminished by an interstellar [extinction factor](:Extinction_%28astronomy%29) of 0.32 due to intervening dust.This system was discovered to be a single-lined [spectroscopic binary](:Spectroscopic_binary) in 1910 by American astronomer [Joseph Haines Moore](:Joseph_Haines_Moore). The pair, component A, orbit each other with a [period](:Orbital_period) of 7.6497 days and an [eccentricity](:Orbital_eccentricity) of 0.35. The primary is a [B-type star](:B-type_star) with a [stellar classification](:Stellar_classification) of B1.5 V or B2 IV, depending on the source. This indicates it may be a [main sequence star](:Main_sequence_star) or a more [evolved](:Stellar_evolution) [subgiant star](:Subgiant_star). It has about 8.1 times the [mass of the Sun](:Solar_mass) and radiates 1,702 times the [solar luminosity](:Solar_luminosity) from its [outer atmosphere](:Stellar_atmosphere) at an [effective temperature](:Effective_temperature) of 20,790 K. It is a hybrid [pulsator](:Stellar_pulsation) and shows spacings in both g and p modes.A third star, component B, is a magnitude 9.38 [F-type main sequence star](:F-type_main_sequence_star) with a classification of F7 V. It has 1.25 times the mass of the Sun and radiates 2.4 times the solar luminosity at an effective temperature of 6,194 K. It lies at an [angular separation](:Angular_separation) of 25.1 [arc seconds](:Arc_second) from the inner pair. They share a [common proper motion](:Common_proper_motion), indicating they may be gravitationally bound with an orbital period of around 41,000 years.The system has a [peculiar velocity](:Peculiar_velocity) of 16.2±4.2 km/s. It belongs to the [Scorpius–Centaurus association](:Scorpius%E2%80%93Centaurus_association) and appears to be a member of the [Gould's Belt](:Gould%27s_Belt)."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
