<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Ross_548> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Ross 548** is a [white dwarf](:White_dwarf) in the [equatorial](:Celestial_equator) [constellation](:Constellation) of [Cetus](:Cetus_%28constellation%29). With a mean [apparent visual magnitude](:Apparent_visual_magnitude) of 14.2 it is much too faint to be visible to the naked eye. Based on [parallax](:Stellar_parallax) measurements, it is located at a distance of 107 [light years](:Light_year) from the [Sun](:Sun). It was found to be [variable](:Variable_star) in 1970 and in 1972 it was given the [variable star designation](:Variable_star_designation) **ZZ Ceti**. This is a [pulsating white dwarf](:Pulsating_white_dwarf) of the DAV type that is the prototype of the [ZZ Ceti variable](:ZZ_Ceti_variable) class., pp. 891, 895.This [DA-class](:White_dwarf_spectroscopy) white dwarf is the surviving core of a [red giant star](:Red_giant_star) that ceased nuclear fusion while shedding its outer envelope. It has a (presumably) homogeneous [core](:Stellar_core) of carbon and oxygen, a relatively thin outer envelope of hydrogen, and a helium mantle. The object has 65% of the [mass of the Sun](:Mass_of_the_Sun), with 1.2% of the [Sun's radius](:Sun%27s_radius). It is radiating 0.3% of the [luminosity of the Sun](:Luminosity_of_the_Sun) at an [effective temperature](:Effective_temperature) of 12,281 K. Ross 548 is spinning with a period of ~38 hours. The dominant pulsation mode of this object has a period of 213.1326 seconds. It has up to 11 known pulsation modes in total."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
